-- THE "FEATURE PRESENT, UNRELATED CODE STILL BEHAVES" CELL for the identity
-- narrowing added to `resolveFieldAmbiguous`'s concrete-receiver arm.  It must be
-- green BEFORE and AFTER that change; that is the property, not a drain.
--
-- ⚠️ WHAT IT IS FOR.  The narrowing runs on EVERY `.field` access whose receiver
-- is concrete and whose field name is declared by more than one record — not only
-- on the colliding ones.  Nothing in this graph collides: three distinct record
-- types, three distinct constructors, three distinct modules.  The only thing
-- shared is the FIELD name `tag`, which is legal and common, and is exactly what
-- routes an ordinary projection onto the edited arm.
--
-- The four cells, and which path each takes:
--
--   `mkA.tag`    — head `Alpha`, registry key `MkAlpha` (a named-field variant is
--                  keyed by its CONSTRUCTOR), so the exact lookup misses and no
--                  key suffix-matches `__Alpha`.  Three owners reach the
--                  multi-owner arm: THIS is the cell that traverses the new code.
--                  Before the change the answer came from `headL` of the
--                  sort-ordered owner keys; after it, from the receiver's own
--                  declaration identity.  Both name `MkAlpha`, so the cell is
--                  green either way — which is the assertion.
--   `mkA.onlyA`  — one owner; the untouched `[r]` singleton arm.
--   `mkB.onlyB`  — one owner, different module; the same untouched arm, proving
--                  the narrowing did not capture the single-owner path.
--   `lv.own`     — `Local` is declared by THIS module.  A module never sees its
--                  own records in the identity companion (it is grown from
--                  `pubDecls` at the END of a module's arm), and `Local` is not
--                  public at all, so this cell stands over the case where no
--                  identity companion row can exist.  One owner, `[r]` arm.
--
-- 🚨 `mkB.tag` IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT.  It is NOT a bystander: at the base of this
-- change it resolves to `MkAlpha` (the first owner key in sort order) and the
-- program is REFUSED with `Type mismatch: Beta vs Alpha`.  That is the #1216 /
-- #1382 defect itself, not unrelated code, so pinning it here would make this a
-- drain cell and destroy the before-AND-after property the cell exists for.
--
-- Hand-derived answer, from the language semantics rather than from the tool:
-- `mkA.tag` = 1, `mkA.onlyA` = 2, `mkB.onlyB` = 4, `lv.own` = 6 → `1,2,4,6`.
import alib.{Alpha, mkA}
import blib.{Beta, mkB}

data Local =
  | MkLocal { tag : Int, own : Int }

lv : Local
lv = MkLocal { tag = 5, own = 6 }

main : <IO> Unit
main =
  putStrLn
    (intToString mkA.tag
      ++ ","
      ++ intToString mkA.onlyA
      ++ ","
      ++ intToString mkB.onlyB
      ++ ","
      ++ intToString lv.own)
